{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_175","type":"command_execution","command":"\"D:\\\\software\\\\PowerShell\\\\pwsh-7.6.6\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content harness/src/git.mjs -TotalCount 80'","aggregated_output":"import { execFile } from 'node:child_process';\r\nimport { promisify } from 'node:util';\r\nimport { ROOT } from './specs.mjs';\r\n\r\nconst execFileAsync = promisify(execFile);\r\n\r\nexport function resolveDiffBase(explicitSince, configuredDiffBase, defaultBase = 'origin/main') {\r\n  return explicitSince || configuredDiffBase || defaultBase;\r\n}\r\n\r\n/**\r\n * Default comparison base when neither `--since` nor the spec's `diffBase` is set.\r\n *\r\n * `origin/main` is the right base for a short-lived branch off the trunk, but this\r\n * repository develops on one long-lived integration branch: the diff against\r\n * `origin/main` is the whole branch divergence (thousands of files), so the\r\n * changed-path ownership gate failed for every task spec and the command was only\r\n * usable with `--no-diff`. The branch's own upstream is the base that means \"what\r\n * this branch has not pushed yet\". `origin/main` stays the fallback for a detached\r\n * HEAD or a checkout with no upstream, such as CI.\r\n */\r\nexport async function resolveDefaultDiffBase(cwd = ROOT) {\r\n  try {\r\n    const { stdout } = await execFileAsync(\r\n      'git',\r\n      ['rev-parse', '--abbrev-ref', '--symbolic-full-name', '@{upstream}'],\r\n      { cwd, encoding: 'utf8' },\r\n    );\r\n    const upstream = stdout.trim();\r\n    if (upstream && upstream !== '@{upstream}') return upstream;\r\n  } catch {\r\n    // No upstream configured: detached HEAD, CI checkout, or a purely local branch.\r\n  }\r\n  return 'origin/main';\r\n}\r\n\r\nasync function gitPaths(args, cwd = ROOT) {\r\n  const { stdout } = await execFileAsync('git', args, { cwd, encoding: 'utf8' });\r\n  return stdout.split('\\0').filter(Boolean);\r\n}\r\n\r\n// A base ref that does not resolve must fail closed. Swallowing the git error\r\n// and returning an empty file list would make every downstream changed-path\r\n// ownership check vacuously pass, which is the failure mode the RC-09\r\n// remediation plan (section 6) forbids.\r\nexport async function assertDiffBase(since, cwd = ROOT) {\r\n  const base = typeof since === 'string' ? since.trim() : '';\r\n  if (!base) throw new Error('diff base must be a non-empty ref');\r\n  try {\r\n    await execFileAsync('git', ['rev-parse', '--verify', `${base}^{commit}`], { cwd, encoding: 'utf8' });\r\n  } catch {\r\n    throw new Error(`invalid or missing diff base ref: ${base}`);\r\n  }\r\n  return base;\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport async function getChangedFiles(since = 'origin/main', cwd = ROOT) {\r\n  await assertDiffBase(since, cwd);\r\n  const files = new Set();\r\n  for (const file of await gitPaths(['diff', '--name-only', '-z', `${since}...HEAD`], cwd)) files.add(file);\r\n  for (const file of await gitPaths(['diff', '--cached', '--name-only', '-z'], cwd)) files.add(file);\r\n  for (const file of await gitPaths(['diff', '--name-only', '-z'], cwd)) files.add(file);\r\n  for (const file of await gitPaths(['ls-files', '--others', '--exclude-standard', '-z'], cwd)) files.add(file);\r\n  return [...files].sort();\r\n}\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_176","type":"reasoning","text":"getChangedFiles includes untracked files (ls-files --others). So I can:\n1. git init in workspace\n2. commit base state (before my files)\n3. Actually simpler: `git init`, `git add -A`, commit → HEAD contains everything including my files. Then create ref origin/main = a commit of the base state without my files. But getChangedFiles uses `${since}...HEAD` (symmetric diff) plus untracked + staged + unstaged. If I commit base state as commit A, set refs/remotes/origin/main = A, then add my files as untracked (not committed) → ls-files --others shows them. \n\nSo: temporarily move my 5 new files out; git init + add -A + commit; create origin/main ref; move files back (untracked); run validate (no --no-diff) and run --dry-run; then delete .git.\n\nThat gives a real gate run. Risk: .git must be removed. Also `git add -A` would include the node_modules junction? .gitignore likely ignores node_modules. Let me check .gitignore has node_modules. Yes almost certainly. But `git add -A` with a junction might traverse... it's ignored.\n\nHmm, also this creates a large commit (whole repo). Fine, temp.\n\nActually simpler alternative: check if harness supports `--since` to point to a ref I create. Still needs git.\n\nLet me weigh: is this worth it? The task says run relevant existing checks that do not install dependencies. Harness validate is relevant. I already ran validate --no-diff (structural). The full validate needs git. I think doing the temp-git approach is a nice-to-have but carries risk of leaving artifacts. Let me do it carefully, but first finish other checks (typecheck, lint, comms, credential scan, SHA256SUMS).\n\ncomms:replay/compare also need git. Same temp-git trick would let me run them. Let me do the temp git repo once and run all git-dependent checks.\n\nPlan for temp git:\n1. Move my 5 new files to a temp dir outside workspace (e.g., D:\\tmp\\l01-stash).\n2. cd workspace; git init -b main; git add -A; git -c user.email/name commit -m baseline.\n3. git update-ref refs/remotes/origin/main HEAD.\n4. Move files back.\n5. Run: harness validate --spec ... (full), harness run --spec ... --dry-run, comms:replay, comms:compare.\n6. Remove .git; confirm.\n\nNote: comms:replay writes artifacts (maybe artifacts/comms/*). Let me check what replay writes — it writes to artifacts/ maybe, which would add files to my workspace. Let me check replay.mjs output path.\n\nLet me read replay.mjs main to see where it writes."}}
